C = [
("h1", "5. Egypt"),
("p", "Claim: Egypt has more oracles than any other nation, in three prophets, and they do not say one thing. Isaiah 19 and 20 give a sentence of civil strife, a hard master, a dried river and a captivity, and then, after the words “in that day,” a blessing in which Egypt is called “my people.” Jeremiah 46 gives the Babylonian stroke and adds, “afterward it shall be inhabited.” Ezekiel 29 to 32 gives a desolation of forty years, a return, a base kingdom, and, in one dated verse, a correction of the first account of what Nebuchadrezzar would receive. The text itself marks which parts have been carried out and which have not. The volume classes Egypt as part-fulfilled, with three clauses held open: Isaiah 19:18–25, Jeremiah 46:26 and Ezekiel 29:13–15."),
("sub", "5.1 The burden of Egypt"),
("p", "Isaiah’s oracle opens with the LORD’s coming: “Behold, the LORD rideth upon a swift cloud, and shall come into Egypt: and the idols of Egypt shall be moved at his presence, and the heart of Egypt shall melt in the midst of it” (Isaiah 19:1). The first sentence is on the gods and on the people’s courage. Then the strife: “And I will set the Egyptians against the Egyptians: and they shall fight every one against his brother, and every one against his neighbour; city against city, and kingdom against kingdom” (Isaiah 19:2). Then the master: “And the Egyptians will I give over into the hand of a cruel lord; and a fierce king shall rule over them, saith the Lord, the LORD of hosts” (Isaiah 19:4). Verses 5 to 10 take the river: the waters “shall fail,” the reeds wither, the fishers mourn, and the weavers of fine flax are confounded."),
("p", "The counsel of the state fails with the river. “Surely the princes of Zoan are fools, the counsel of the wise counsellors of Pharaoh is become brutish” (Isaiah 19:11). The cause is named and it is the LORD’s: “The LORD hath mingled a perverse spirit in the midst thereof: and they have caused Egypt to err in every work thereof, as a drunken man staggereth in his vomit” (Isaiah 19:14). The first half of the burden ends with the effect on the nation: “In that day shall Egypt be like unto women: and it shall be afraid and fear because of the shaking of the hand of the LORD of hosts” (Isaiah 19:16), and with an unlikely cause of fear: “And the land of Judah shall be a terror unto Egypt, every one that maketh mention thereof shall be afraid in himself, because of the counsel of the LORD of hosts, which he hath determined against it” (Isaiah 19:17)."),
("sub", "5.2 A sign with a date"),
("p", "Isaiah 20 is a short chapter that gives the burden a date and a prophet’s body. “In the year that Tartan came unto Ashdod, (when Sargon the king of Assyria sent him,) and fought against Ashdod, and took it” (Isaiah 20:1). In that year Isaiah was told to loosen his sackcloth and put off his shoe, and he went “naked and barefoot” (Isaiah 20:2), and the LORD named it: “Like as my servant Isaiah hath walked naked and barefoot three years for a sign and wonder upon Egypt and upon Ethiopia” (Isaiah 20:3). The meaning follows: “So shall the king of Assyria lead away the Egyptians prisoners, and the Ethiopians captives, young and old” (Isaiah 20:4). The chapter closes with the voice of the coastland that had looked to Egypt for help: “Behold, such is our expectation, whither we flee for help to be delivered from the king of Assyria: and how shall we escape?” (Isaiah 20:6)."),
("p", "The chapter is a sentence on Egypt and on those who trusted Egypt, and it is written as a sign acted out. The Bible does not narrate the captivity it foretells. The volume reports the sentence and the sign, and it classes the Assyrian stroke with the sentences the text states but does not itself report finished."),
("sub", "5.3 In that day"),
("p", "The verses that follow the burden are the ones the argument turns on. They do not begin with a new heading. They begin in the same sentence, with the same words: “In that day shall five cities in the land of Egypt speak the language of Canaan, and swear to the LORD of hosts; one shall be called, The city of destruction” (Isaiah 19:18). The turn is marked by an expression, “in that day,” which stands in verses 16, 18, 19, 21, 23 and 24. What follows is a list of things that have not been reported anywhere else in Scripture. There is “an altar to the LORD in the midst of the land of Egypt, and a pillar at the border thereof to the LORD” (Isaiah 19:19). There is a sign and a deliverer: “they shall cry unto the LORD because of the oppressors, and he shall send them a saviour, and a great one, and he shall deliver them” (Isaiah 19:20). There is knowledge and worship: “the LORD shall be known to Egypt, and the Egyptians shall know the LORD in that day, and shall do sacrifice and oblation” (Isaiah 19:21). There is both a stroke and a healing: “And the LORD shall smite Egypt: he shall smite and heal it” (Isaiah 19:22)."),
("p", "Then the last two verses of the burden reach beyond Egypt. “In that day shall there be a highway out of Egypt to Assyria, and the Assyrian shall come into Egypt, and the Egyptian into Assyria, and the Egyptians shall serve with the Assyrians” (Isaiah 19:23). “In that day shall Israel be the third with Egypt and with Assyria, even a blessing in the midst of the land” (Isaiah 19:24). The title of each is given: “Blessed be Egypt my people, and Assyria the work of my hands, and Israel mine inheritance” (Isaiah 19:25). Three names, three epithets. The one that is Israel’s is “inheritance.” The first two are phrases that the prophets elsewhere use of Israel, and here they are given to Egypt and Assyria without being taken from Israel."),
("p", "The volume holds this passage open, as a future blessing of Egypt and Assyria in the Kingdom of Israel’s restored line, and not as fulfilled in an earlier age or in the church. It rests the judgment on what the text requires. The passage needs a day when Egypt and Assyria both worship the LORD, when there is a highway between them, when they serve together, and when Israel stands with them as a third, a “blessing in the midst of the land.” It needs Israel to be there. No earlier age the volume knows has all of these together, and none of the later passages of Scripture reports them done. The volume does not claim to date the day. It claims only that the text says it is a day to come, introduced by the same words the prophets use for the day of the LORD."),
("sub", "5.4 Jeremiah: Carchemish and what follows"),
("p", "Jeremiah’s oracle against Egypt opens with a date given by an event. It is “Against Egypt, against the army of Pharaoh-necho king of Egypt, which was by the river Euphrates in Carchemish, which Nebuchadrezzar king of Babylon smote in the fourth year of Jehoiakim the son of Josiah king of Judah” (Jeremiah 46:2). The first stroke is therefore reported inside the heading, as a thing that had happened. The second word is a further one: “how Nebuchadrezzar king of Babylon should come and smite the land of Egypt” (Jeremiah 46:13). The figures are of a heifer and of a flood (46:7, 8, 20), and the voices that say what was left are of Pharaoh: “Pharaoh king of Egypt is but a noise; he hath passed the time appointed” (Jeremiah 46:17)."),
("p", "The sentence is plain and it names the gods. “Behold, I will punish the multitude of No, and Pharaoh, and Egypt, with their gods, and their kings; even Pharaoh, and all them that trust in him” (Jeremiah 46:25). And then the clause that does what Moab’s and Ammon’s do: “And I will deliver them into the hand of those that seek their lives, and into the hand of Nebuchadrezzar king of Babylon, and into the hand of his servants: and afterward it shall be inhabited, as in the days of old, saith the LORD” (Jeremiah 46:26). It stands in the same verse as the stroke. Verses 27 and 28 turn to Jacob: “Fear not thou, O my servant Jacob,” because, as the LORD says, “I will make a full end of all the nations whither I have driven thee: but I will not make a full end of thee, but correct thee in measure” (Jeremiah 46:27, 28). The verses about Egypt and the verses to Jacob are set together at the close of the chapter, and the same relation holds here that held in the cup of Jeremiah 25: Israel’s correction and the nations’ judgment run side by side."),
("sub", "5.5 Ezekiel: forty years, a base kingdom, and a correction"),
("p", "Ezekiel’s Egypt oracles fill chapters 29 to 32. They open with Pharaoh, “the great dragon that lieth in the midst of his rivers, which hath said, My river is mine own, and I have made it for myself” (Ezekiel 29:3), and the charge of the weakness he showed to Israel: “because they have been a staff of reed to the house of Israel” (Ezekiel 29:6). The sentence names a term. The land shall be “utterly waste and desolate,” and “neither shall it be inhabited forty years” (Ezekiel 29:10, 11); the Egyptians shall be scattered “among the nations” (Ezekiel 29:12). Then the clause: “Yet thus saith the Lord GOD; At the end of forty years will I gather the Egyptians from the people whither they were scattered” (Ezekiel 29:13). “And I will bring again the captivity of Egypt, and will cause them to return into the land of Pathros, into the land of their habitation; and they shall be there a base kingdom” (Ezekiel 29:14). “It shall be the basest of the kingdoms; neither shall it exalt itself any more above the nations” (Ezekiel 29:15). And the effect on Israel: “And it shall be no more the confidence of the house of Israel” (Ezekiel 29:16)."),
("p", "The clause of return is of a particular kind. It restores Egypt and it keeps Egypt low. The volume reads the base kingdom of verse 14 and the blessing of Isaiah 19:25 as two statements about the same nation that do not cancel each other. A nation can be the basest of the kingdoms and still be named “my people.” The text says both, and the volume holds both as written. Readers differ on whether the forty years of Ezekiel 29:11 and 12 are a count of years or a round number, and the volume reports the figure as written and states no date for it."),
("p", "Then comes the passage this volume has named already. The date is later than the others: “in the seven and twentieth year, in the first month, in the first day of the month” (Ezekiel 29:17). Nebuchadrezzar “caused his army to serve a great service against Tyrus: every head was made bald, and every shoulder was peeled: yet had he no wages, nor his army, for Tyrus, for the service that he had served against it” (Ezekiel 29:18). The LORD’s answer is the substitution: “Behold, I will give the land of Egypt unto Nebuchadrezzar king of Babylon; and he shall take her multitude, and take her spoil, and take her prey; and it shall be the wages for his army” (Ezekiel 29:19), and “I have given him the land of Egypt for his labour wherewith he served against it, because they wrought for me, saith the Lord GOD” (Ezekiel 29:20). The chapter closes with a clause for Israel: “In that day will I cause the horn of the house of Israel to bud forth” (Ezekiel 29:21). This is the text reporting its own correction; chapter 6 reads it with Tyre."),
("p", "Chapter 30 keeps to Egypt. “For the day is near, even the day of the LORD is near, a cloudy day; it shall be the time of the heathen” (Ezekiel 30:3). The instrument is named again: “I will also make the multitude of Egypt to cease by the hand of Nebuchadrezzar king of Babylon” (Ezekiel 30:10). The figure of the arm repeats it: “Son of man, I have broken the arm of Pharaoh king of Egypt” (Ezekiel 30:21), and “I will strengthen the arms of the king of Babylon, and the arms of Pharaoh shall fall down” (Ezekiel 30:25). The chapter ends where the earlier ended, with a scattering: “And I will scatter the Egyptians among the nations, and disperse them among the countries; and they shall know that I am the LORD” (Ezekiel 30:26). Chapter 32 continues the lament and the volume does not quote it."),
("ctbl", "Egypt in four questions.", [
    ("Question", "The text", "Passage"),
    ("Against whom?", "Egypt, Pharaoh, Egypt’s princes and gods, and those who trusted Egypt", "Isaiah 19:1; 20:5, 6; Jeremiah 46:25; Ezekiel 29:2"),
    ("What ground?", "Idols; the claim “My river is mine own”; a staff of reed to Israel", "Isaiah 19:1, 3; Ezekiel 29:3, 6"),
    ("What term and instrument?", "The king of Assyria (Isaiah 20:4); Nebuchadrezzar (Jeremiah 46:13; Ezekiel 30:10); forty years’ desolation (Ezekiel 29:11)", "Isaiah 20; Jeremiah 46; Ezekiel 29–30"),
    ("What the text reports done", "The first stroke at Carchemish (Jeremiah 46:2); the wages given in place of Tyre’s (Ezekiel 29:19, 20)", "Jeremiah 46:2; Ezekiel 29:17–20"),
    ("What stays open", "“afterward it shall be inhabited”; the gathering after forty years; the blessing of Egypt, Assyria and Israel", "Jeremiah 46:26; Ezekiel 29:13–15; Isaiah 19:18–25"),
    ("Class", "Part-fulfilled, with three open clauses", "Isaiah 19; Jeremiah 46; Ezekiel 29–30"),
  ], [0.22, 0.48, 0.3]),
("sub", "5.6 What the volume takes from the passages"),
("bul", [
    "Egypt is judged in three prophets, with the LORD’s coming, civil strife, a hard master and a dried river (Isaiah 19:1–15), an Assyrian captivity acted out by Isaiah (Isaiah 20), the Babylonian stroke (Jeremiah 46) and a desolation of forty years (Ezekiel 29).",
    "The text reports some of it done, in the heading of Jeremiah 46:2 and in the substitution of Ezekiel 29:17–20; it does not narrate the rest.",
    "Three clauses of restoration are attached to Egypt: Jeremiah 46:26, Ezekiel 29:13–15 and Isaiah 19:18–25.",
    "Isaiah 19:18–25 is held open as a future blessing of Egypt and Assyria with Israel as the third, in the Kingdom of Israel’s restored line, and is not read as already fulfilled or as the church.",
    "The base kingdom of Ezekiel 29:14, 15 and the blessing of Isaiah 19:25 are two statements about one nation, and the volume holds both as written.",
  ]),
]
